[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one parser fix queued. Its <ProblemCount ticket={4471} />
reports hold <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="problems" /> problems. The queued fix closes one of them.
Each problem now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix does not close #4471. After it ships, <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports
from the booking-v3 form and <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day-move" /> one-day moves are still
open.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Every number here comes from `reports-4471.json` at render time, through `problems.ts`, counted the
way the desk's own view counts: a report counts where `causes.ts` files it. The export has some
reports twice; one id counts once.

### parser · <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" of="link" />

The shared parser reads every date month first. All our tenants write the day first, so any date
with a day of 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped: 7 April goes out as 4 July. This is
the bug #4471 was opened for, and the queued fix. It reaches <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" of="tenants" />
tenants through the app, email and the booking-v2 form.

### hint · <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" of="link" />

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint tells them,
month first, and the form reads the field the tenant's way, day first. The symptom is the same swap,
so the reports look like the parser bug, but the fix is in the form. A parser fix does not touch it.
Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants (<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" of="tenants" />
tenants) land here, whatever intake tagged them.

### one-day-move · <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day-move" of="link" />

These dates are not swapped. They move by one day: Tuesday 6 October shows as Monday, a client in
Lisbon gets Tuesday while Toronto gets Monday, an invite arrives before the booking was made. The
desk already read them this way (`desk-notes.json`), and `CauseTable` footnotes them under `parser`,
where they are filed and still counted. That points at time zone handling, not the parser, so they
need their own ticket. They count there too.

## Why one ticket hid three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match.
A subject line says what the reporter saw, not why it happened:

<SubjectSpread ticket={4471} />

Intake's tag does not split them either. It is a guess from the reporter's browser; the problem
depends on the tenant and the way the report came in (see the "intake tagged" column above).

## What changes

- Engineering: three tickets, three fixes. Close each on its own.
- Desk: while #4471 stays merged, new reports go to the ticket of their problem, not to #4471.
  Use `problemsOf` in `src/lib/kb/problems.ts`; a matching subject line is not enough.
- #4471's page still says "one issue". Leave it to its owner; this page is the split.

